WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.671 --> 00:00:03.440
- Hi everyone, welcome to
the Homeroom Livestream,

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.000
Sal here from Khan Academy,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.890
we have a very exciting guest today.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:10.710
We have Tabatha Rosproy 2020
National Teacher of the Year.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.950
So if you have questions
for what it's like

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.310
to be a teacher, especially
a teacher of early learners,

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.420
start putting them on the message boards

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.260
on Facebook and YouTube.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.110
But before we jump into what promises

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:22.790
to be a really fun conversation,

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.130
I'll make my standard
announcements reminder.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.450
The Khan Academy is a
not-for-profit organization,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.560
we can only exist because
of philanthropic donations

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.790
from folks like yourself.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:33.400
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.710
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.370
Also wanna make a special shout out

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.460
to several organizations that stepped up

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.620
when they found out that we
were running at a deficit

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.650
going into COVID and that
deficit has only accelerated

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.310
because of our costs going up
because of our server costs.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.890
We're trying to accelerate
a whole bunch of programs

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.120
for teachers, parents, and students.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.740
So special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.310
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.930
I still also wanna remind folks

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:03.140
about Homeroom with Sal, the podcast.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.910
That's essentially a modified
version of the livestream,

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.200
kind of some of the more poignant moments

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.310
that you might find useful

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.750
and you can consume it
safely in your car wherever

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.540
without having to see things.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.430
So I encourage you to go subscribe

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.890
to the Homeroom, the podcast.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.410
So with that, I'm excited
to have Tabatha Rosproy on,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.280
Tabatha, good to see you.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.630
- Hi, good to see you
too, thanks for having me.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.620
- No, it's my honor,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.410
you are 2020 National Teacher of the Year,

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.080
you're based in Kansas.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:42.080
So how did you become
National Teacher of the Year?

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.140
- I ask myself--
- What is the process like?

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.490
- So I was nominated at my district level

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.410
for State Teacher of the Year,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.270
and you go to a regional banquet,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.900
there are four regions in Kansas,

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.480
and I was named one of the
finalists here in Kansas,

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.460
and then I was named
Kansas Teacher of the Year

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.120
shortly after that, and after
that you submit an application

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.320
to CCSSO, the Council of
Chief State School Officers,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.560
and they have a team of
17 different organizations

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.660
that pick four finalists,
and I was one of those,

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:15.470
I was really surprised
when I got the phone call

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.570
because it was a Washington D.C. number,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.490
and I thought it was a political call,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:20.800
just going into an election year.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.480
So I didn't answer at first
before I got a text message

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.120
asking me to call them back.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.880
And after that the other three
finalists who are incredible,

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.310
we all went to Washington
D.C. and interviewed

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:37.330
with those varying education
organizations, we did keynotes,

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.080
we interviewed with press
and just a short while later,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.430
I was selected as National
Teacher of the Year.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.155
- Wow, well, congratulations.

00:02:46.155 --> 00:02:48.320
I was lucky to see some video footage

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.960
of some of the things you
do with your students.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.690
And I didn't see what the
other teachers are doing,

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.180
but it made me pretty convinced

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.380
that you are very well deserving

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.420
of National Teacher of the Year.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.330
I think we have some of this footage,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.390
if we could take a look at it fully pay,

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.630
and if you could talk us
through what's going on.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.730
- Oh yes, this is a Zoom call

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.110
with a class full of preschoolers,

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.920
which I'm sure some
people can identify with.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.630
The older kids, you can't get to talk,

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.710
but the younger kids, you
can't get them to stop talking.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:20.710
So it's incredibly unique
to be on video chat

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.860
with four year olds.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.900
And I think at this time I
was just reading them a story

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.540
about why they couldn't be at school.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.210
This was really shortly
after state school buildings

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.453
had been shutdown in the spring.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.560
Oh, and this is one of our
Friday dance parties here.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.050
- Every Friday?

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.350
- Yes, every Friday we have a dance party

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:48.360
and other occasionally in
between when the students

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.460
ask for them or we just need a break,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.450
I have a disco light that
I've got pretty cheap online,

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:57.630
and we play oldies, we
play things that they like

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.140
from their favorite YouTube stars

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.780
and we have a great time
celebrating together.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.910
- I hope to attend one day
this seems like a better party

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.741
than most of the things I'm invited to.

00:04:06.741 --> 00:04:09.430
(laughing)

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.120
- Oh, and here is we were
releasing some of our butterflies.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.500
So this is outside of the nursing home

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.650
that my preschool is housed inside of.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:19.450
And what you can't see
is kind of off screen.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.530
We have some grandma
and grandpa residents.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.640
That's what we call the
volunteers who live there

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.960
watching us release our butterflies

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.273
that we watched grow from caterpillars.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.213
- Wow.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.150
- On here these are my students,

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.140
so one thing I really
focus on in my classroom

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:40.900
is social emotional health
and self regulation,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.290
and those relationships
from student to student,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.170
and here we are doing an activity

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:46.560
that builds those relationships,

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.860
where they're doing a row,
row, row, your boat game,

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.860
they have to keep each other
safe, it's playful, it's silly,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.750
there's positive physical touch

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.020
and just loving interactions

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.790
and that helps them grow
closer to each other

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:00.940
and build those super
strong relationships.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.170
- Wow, no, I'm, envious of your students.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.860
I mean, I had some great teachers too,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.650
but I don't remember the dance parties

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.380
or the row, row, row,
your boat simulations

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.770
and the butterflies.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:17.460
That's incredible.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.170
And the age group you
teach out there in Kansas,

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:22.180
you teach, you said preschoolers
mainly four year olds.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.660
- Yeah I teach four year old preschool,

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.890
yeah my classroom is
funded by the public school

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.390
and special education and at-risk dollars

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.290
but yeah we're a public school
program for four year olds.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:37.090
- And what kind of gravitated
you to that age group?

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:40.100
And what do you think
is kind of the mindset

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.507
or the special sauce that
you bring day in, day out

00:05:42.507 --> 00:05:44.670
and I'm sure many other
teachers do as well,

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.560
but you clearly are one of those teachers

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.100
that I think these four year olds,

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.200
even when they're 40 or 50,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.190
are gonna remember those moments.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.610
What drew you to teaching
and especially that group

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:56.880
and how do you view it that gives you

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.493
that special pixie dust?

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.370
- When I was in high school,

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.370
I knew that I wanted to help people.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:07.320
But I began taking the Dual
Credit College Spanish class

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.980
in which I got to go down to the preschool

00:06:10.815 --> 00:06:13.420
on our block, like in our district

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.870
and teach Spanish to preschoolers.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.700
And before we started
interacting with the students,

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.240
we got to just observe in the classroom.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.800
And I remember this moment so clearly,

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.110
it was the first time I
had ever witnessed teaching

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.740
from an outsider perspective.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:33.310
I had always been on the end
of being delivered instruction.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:35.780
And it was the first time
that I truly saw teaching

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.940
as the art form that it is.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.390
That teacher that I was
watching her name is Pat Walton

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.600
and she is now retired, but the way

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.650
that she connected with her students,

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.740
that she really acted as a
guide for their learning,

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.570
the way that she built
relationships with them,

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.510
it was magical for me to see.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.520
And from then on out, I knew that I wanted

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.190
to work with kids, and
I knew that I wanted

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:56.940
to work with really young kids too.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:06:59.600
- Well, that's incredible.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.150
And there's tons of questions coming in

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:01.983
because I think especially--

00:07:01.983 --> 00:07:03.120
- Okay.
- Young kid crowd...

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.500
My youngest is five and a half,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.150
so I won't make too many of the questions

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.530
about my personal help that I need,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.530
but from YouTube, we have a
question from Carris Alexander.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.460
What tips do you have for older siblings

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.720
trying to help younger
siblings with tutoring

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.340
and homework in distance learning?

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:20.183
How much should we help and how?

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:22.380
- That is a great question.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.190
I have so many students in my class

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:26.930
who you can tell when they haven't had

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.640
to like talk for themselves
or do things for themselves.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.790
And usually they have older siblings.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:33.890
So what I would do is
just have a conversation

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.200
with your older kids and talk to them

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.980
about the role that they play, right?

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.730
So they wanna take care of them

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:41.410
but the best way that we can take care

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.730
of our younger siblings,
is to help them learn

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:45.450
how to do things for themselves.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.770
Because we won't always be there, right?

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:51.520
And they need to be able to
solve problems on their own.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.710
And so you could give
them a list of things

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.990
that they could do to be helpful,

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:58.090
including being a good role model,

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.610
like what does it look
like when you are doing

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.150
your own work, include your
younger sibling in that time.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.630
But I would just be really
open with them about that

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.740
and be thankful for the
moments that they are helping,

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.580
because you definitely
don't wanna see them

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.720
go the other way and not be
interested in that at all.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.580
But I think for sure, older
siblings can be great models,

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.940
but you're right on that
sometimes we give them

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.290
a little bit too much help.

00:08:22.588 --> 00:08:24.220
- So help them help themselves.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.590
And I've gotta believe that Carris

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.380
is a pretty good older sibling.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.430
The very fact that they're
asking this question

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:30.530
shows that they care a lot

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:33.520
so your younger siblings
are very lucky Carris.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.007
From YouTube Miss Victoria asks,

00:08:37.007 --> 00:08:39.417
"How do you react to sassy kids?

00:08:39.417 --> 00:08:42.230
"The ones that say "You
can't tell me what to do",

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.660
et cetera."

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:45.060
I've never encountered sassy kids.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.990
My kids never do anything like that,

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:49.400
but I guess in theory, other kids might.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.313
So I'd love to be advised there too.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:54.910
- I think that one of the
first things you have to do

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:58.020
as the adult in the situation
is recognize your triggers.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.380
And so that definitely would
have been one of my triggers

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.530
being talked to disrespectfully

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.870
because when I was a child
that did not fly in our house,

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.550
so now I see how that triggers me

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.310
because I'm super aware of it

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.880
but what I would say is when that happens,

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.560
your child or the child
you're working with

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.860
is probably not in a state of their brain

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.720
where they can make good decisions

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.360
and be a good friend and
be kind and respectful.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.760
So you're gonna need to help
them sort of co-regulate

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:25.670
in that moment.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.710
And so that means if
you react aggressively,

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.030
they're probably gonna
react aggressively too,

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.930
because aggression begets aggression.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:36.680
So what you're gonna wanna
do is to take a deep breath,

00:09:37.791 --> 00:09:39.380
and I just say to myself,

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.570
this behavior is information, right?

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.740
And I didn't make that phrase
up, it's not unique to me.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.900
But when we view behavior as information

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.750
and not as our students or our kids

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:51.460
being out to get us or
trying to push our buttons,

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:52.980
then we are more likely to be able

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.140
to come up with a solution that is helpful

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.240
rather than hurtful to them and to us

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.340
and to our relationship.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.420
And so when someone says
something super sassy to me,

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.210
I might just take a deep breath

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.550
and give myself a second before I respond,

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.890
because sometimes they are looking

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:09.750
for a quick response from you.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.100
But I would just say,
you can either address

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.050
that hurtful statement, I don't like it

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.823
when you talk that way to me.

00:10:15.823 --> 00:10:19.900
If you wanna speak to me,
you can use kind words like,

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.370
and give them an example,

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.350
or I can tell you're really frustrated,

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:25.160
I'm gonna give you a
few minutes to calm down

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:26.360
and then I'll come back.

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:28.910
- That's powerful advice,

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.810
not just for dealing with young kids.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:34.260
I would say any relationship
feelings are real,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:35.330
don't escalate--

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:36.460
- Yeah.
- Try to understand

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.057
where they're coming from.

00:10:38.057 --> 00:10:40.609
Just to add to that, I
mean I was joking obviously

00:10:40.609 --> 00:10:43.280
I am always trying to navigate.

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:45.990
And I think I fall into
that trap as a parent does,

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.650
if like your child is
not eating or whatever,

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:49.540
and you're trying to push them

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.660
and then they kinda dig in their heels,

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.160
and I think they're trying to
push boundaries a little bit.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:57.230
And especially if things
get really fall apart,

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:58.110
they're having a tantrum

00:10:58.110 --> 00:10:59.980
or maybe you feel like having a tantrum,

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:00.990
how do you deal with that?

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.010
I can imagine, especially
the four year old crowd,

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:04.180
sometimes with my five-year-old,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.240
he just falls apart and we're trying

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:08.490
to put him back toge...

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:09.813
How do you deal with that?

00:11:11.367 --> 00:11:13.040
- Tantrums are a hard, right?

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.820
And my heart goes out to people

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.570
when I see their kids doing it in public,

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:18.430
because I know they're so embarrassed,

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:19.640
but what you just need to remember

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.810
is we've all been through that,
we've all gone through that,

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:24.990
and if people are judging
you for when that happens,

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:26.650
then just, you can ignore them.

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:29.430
But again, I would say remaining calm

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.540
is the most important
thing in that moment.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:34.720
So sometimes it will look
like me asking a child,

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:38.500
if it's okay, if I can
hug them or hold them

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.820
or hold their hands and just
doing some deep breaths.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.170
Because at that moment,
you can't reason with them,

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:46.440
so they're just different
areas of your brain,

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.110
and usually when they're
throwing a tantrum

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:49.990
they're in a lower center of their brain,

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:53.790
where they can't reason and
they're in fight or flight mode

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:56.700
and probably fight if
they're in that tantrum mode.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.120
And so you would just wanna breathe,

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:02.110
and you calmly breathing
not engaging verbally,

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.130
usually will calm the situation enough

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.150
where you can get to a point

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.443
that you can reason with that child.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:10.570
- Wow that's masterful.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:14.380
I feel more empowered
just hearing you say that.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.460
So a lot of questions
coming in from YouTube,

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.007
Hush Gotham asks,

00:12:19.007 --> 00:12:21.367
"How do you feel when a small
kid asks a lot of questions,

00:12:21.367 --> 00:12:22.517
"how do you deal with them?

00:12:22.517 --> 00:12:24.097
"What to do when a kid asks a question

00:12:24.097 --> 00:12:26.960
"and you don't know the answer back."

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.500
- So this happens to me all the time.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.640
I always say, you never
know what you don't know

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.210
until a kid asks you to explain something.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.420
And so like our high school mascot

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:36.980
where I live is the Vikings.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:39.240
And someone asked me what's a Viking.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:43.270
And I was like, how do I
explain this to a child?

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:44.610
What a Viking is.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:48.890
And once someone asked me,
like, what is chocolate?

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.330
Or just something like I was like,

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:51.360
I really gotta go through this.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.120
So first I would encourage
you to follow up,

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:55.690
and I think that it's okay to admit

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:57.050
that you don't know something,

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:59.410
so when a student asks me a question,

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:00.550
and I don't know, I'd be like,

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:01.970
let's look that up together.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.300
Where do you think we can
find out what that is?

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:05.670
And so you can teach them

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.980
how to do these beginning
steps of research.

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.320
But I think also when they ask you

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.410
something that you don't know,

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.030
ask them what they think it is,

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:16.710
and see if you can get an
explanation from there,

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.380
kinda turn it on them and maybe start

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:21.643
working on some of those
critical thinking skills.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.520
- It's interesting that
you brought up Vikings.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.770
I recently read an article
that even our perceptions

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.740
of Vikings, we imagine
these, they're Viking

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.600
from definitely from Scandinavia,

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:34.080
they were the seafarers
who would go and like,

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:37.260
pillage coastal villages
and things like that.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:40.750
But apparently the Vikings
might've been different

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.677
than we imagined, but anyway
that's a whole other topic.

00:13:43.677 --> 00:13:45.881
- We could jus go on and on.

00:13:45.881 --> 00:13:48.120
- Yeah it's fascinating.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:51.050
One question, I mean this
is related to the last one,

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.830
but this is something I
deal with a lot at home

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:55.210
is I have three kids, 11, nine, and five.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.600
You're dealing with classrooms
of many, four year olds.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:13:59.940
one thing that kids like to do

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.240
is they like to show adults that they care

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:03.630
about the things that they've done,

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.780
but you know sometimes
after a long day of work

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:08.910
and I'm sure sometimes
you're tired at work.

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:11.510
You have five kids coming
at two at the same time

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.850
and say, "Hey, Ms. Rosproy
look at what I did,

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.270
look at what I did, did
you know that this is true?

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.580
Did you know that the
Vikings never ate chocolate?

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.460
Whatever, how do you deal with that?

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.760
How do you triage that inbound

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.260
that I think I see a lot
of young kids like to do.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.200
- Oh my gosh yeah they can be exhausting.

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:30.150
I sometimes have bruises
on my shoulders or my legs

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.133
from being tapped so hard.

00:14:32.133 --> 00:14:33.856
(laughing)

00:14:33.856 --> 00:14:35.280
I did make a rule in my class.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.840
If you want my attention,
you need to say my name

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.540
instead of tapping me as hard.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:42.150
And then you just hear
your name constantly.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:46.320
And I would just really try
to create some boundaries

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.780
if you know that the
minute that you get home,

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.870
that you're not gonna
be able to handle this,

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.720
you can create those
boundaries with your kids

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:55.700
and say all right, when I
get home, let's do hugs,

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.390
high fives, kisses,
whatever you're used to.

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:01.580
And then I need 10 minutes
and I'm gonna be in this room

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:03.600
and I'm gonna put a little
stop sign on the door,

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.080
and that means don't come in

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:07.290
and then do what you need to do,

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:10.350
to collect yourself, to
meditate, to exercise,

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:13.070
to check your emails, spend
a little time by yourself

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.550
because it's overwhelming,
that like fatigue

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:18.490
from managing a household
and managing a job.

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.880
And then I would say,
come out and be ready

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.370
to have those discussions.

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.730
And if your child is struggling with that,

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:27.950
I sometimes give kids a notebook

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:29.750
and then they can write or draw

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:31.490
the things they wanna talk to me about.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:33.440
And then later when we have time,

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:34.707
I might point to my schedule and say,

00:15:34.707 --> 00:15:36.750
"Okay, it's time to show me the ideas

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:38.405
that you wanted to share at circle time,

00:15:38.405 --> 00:15:41.000
when it was everyone else's turn to talk."

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:43.860
So I just think creating
some routines right,

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.677
might be helpful in that situation.

00:15:46.894 --> 00:15:48.793
- That's super valuable,
I'm gonna try to apply that.

00:15:48.793 --> 00:15:53.527
There's a related question
from YouTube Death Stroke asks,

00:15:53.527 --> 00:15:56.707
"What if a child asks a question
which shouldn't be answered

00:15:56.707 --> 00:16:00.137
"at the time, like adult
stuff, what'd you do then?"

00:16:01.270 --> 00:16:02.850
- They do, they like know

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:04.910
but what's funny is most of the time

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:06.820
they don't know that it's adult stuff.

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:10.030
And so, sometimes I will even
get questions in my classroom,

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:12.290
like about religion or about,

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:14.100
things that aren't really
my place to comment on,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.410
I mean, even politics
at preschool sometimes.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:19.270
And I try to be just,
you know, really neutral.

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:22.170
And I will say everybody
believes different things.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:23.170
This is something you might wanna

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:24.410
ask your mom and dad about,

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:25.890
or your grandma about your caregiver,

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.490
whoever they live with when you get home.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:30.480
And if you want me to help you do that,

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:31.780
let's write a note to them

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:33.020
and know that you brought that up.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:35.260
But if it's something
kind of inappropriate,

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.637
I think that I might just say,

00:16:37.637 --> 00:16:39.210
"Let's talk about that a little bit later

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:40.750
right now we're talking about math

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:43.710
or right now we're talking about letter A

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:44.543
or something like that.

00:16:44.543 --> 00:16:47.070
I just wouldn't make a big deal about it.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.930
Because once you make a huge deal,

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:52.060
the other kids know that it
was something inappropriate

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:53.280
and probably the pattern is gonna be,

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.940
then other kids will ask about it too.

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:57.000
So just try to be chill.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.083
That would be my advice.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:01.770
- Yeah now great advice.

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:05.537
So from YouTube, Susanna
Garcia Dominguez asks,

00:17:05.537 --> 00:17:06.377
"Hi, Tabatha,

00:17:06.377 --> 00:17:09.820
"how do you teach young
students to regulate?"

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:12.420
- Okay, this is a great question too.

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.370
So I am a huge fan of
conscious discipline.

00:17:15.370 --> 00:17:16.890
And if you've never heard of that,

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.347
I would start by reading the book

00:17:18.347 --> 00:17:22.360
"Easy to Love, Difficult to
Discipline" by Dr. Becky Bailey.

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:24.770
I'm not in any kind of like
partnership or promotion

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:25.603
I just love--

00:17:25.603 --> 00:17:26.547
- Conscious discipline.
- I'm writing that down,

00:17:26.547 --> 00:17:27.531
let me get some paper.

00:17:27.531 --> 00:17:28.364
"Easy...

00:17:28.364 --> 00:17:29.197
What is that again?

00:17:29.197 --> 00:17:30.073
"Easy to Love,

00:17:30.073 --> 00:17:32.210
- " Difficult to Discipline".

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:35.910
That book changed my life
and now I am a huge fan

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:37.670
of Dr. Becky Bailey and
all the work she does,

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:39.210
she has tons of free resources

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.050
on the conscious discipline website

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:43.630
for families and for teachers.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:48.370
And one big thing to do is
first you have to teach them

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:50.950
sort of what feelings are, right?

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:52.150
What's it noticing?

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:53.580
What's it feel like when I'm angry?

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.430
What's it look like when I'm sad?

00:17:55.430 --> 00:17:57.070
What is my body doing?

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.930
You wanna work on helping
them notice things

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:01.460
like that about themselves
and about others.

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:03.250
And a big way that you can do that

00:18:03.250 --> 00:18:05.933
is not using judgemental language like,

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.280
I like it when you're mad

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:10.700
or like it when you're happy and calm,

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:13.030
I don't like it when you're sad or angry

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:14.830
because feelings are okay.

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:18.060
Like what's not okay is
exploding when you're angry.

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.610
But it is okay to be angry,

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.790
your feelings, give us information,

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:24.080
we just need to teach kids what to do.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.500
We need to teach them
those replacement behaviors

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:29.910
for tantrums and for
throwing things or hitting

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:31.710
or hiding things like that.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:33.800
And so again, you wanna
wait until they're calm

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.030
to do the teaching.

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:39.160
You wanna model calming
strategies like deep breathing,

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.730
maybe coloring a picture when
you're feeling overwhelmed

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.800
or getting a hug from
a person that you love.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:47.440
I mean, I can go on and on about this,

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.330
but I think the biggest
part is letting them know

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:51.940
that feelings are okay
and helping them notice

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:52.800
what feelings look like

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.963
and what to do when you're
having those feelings.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:58.530
- Really, I'm gonna buy this book

00:18:58.530 --> 00:18:59.600
right after this conversation.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:00.433
- (indistinct)
- But I love

00:19:00.433 --> 00:19:01.880
what you're saying.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:03.890
That's actually something I've
only recently appreciated,

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:04.950
not just with dealing with kids,

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:06.600
but just relationships in general

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.390
is that you should never
question someone's feelings,

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.500
feelings by definition are real.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:13.730
So you should never
tell someone that like,

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:15.470
they're not real, or you can't have that,

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:18.630
or our perceptions of
reality might be different

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:20.150
and people can be right or wrong

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:21.710
about the perceptions of reality,

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:23.370
but the feelings are real.

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.720
And so, yeah no, I'm
definitely gonna take a look

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:27.330
at that book.

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.150
So this is a question
I've been getting a lot,

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.650
I'd love your advice
cause you deal day in,

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.030
day out with young kids.

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:35.880
From Facebook Irene is asking

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.410
with the short attention
spans that younger kids have,

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:41.043
how do you keep them engaged
when teaching online?

00:19:42.659 --> 00:19:46.490
- This is so hard and I differ
a lot in my school of thought

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:50.840
from what the reality
that many of our schools

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:52.310
are in right now.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:55.960
I think five to 10 minutes max

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.840
is the attention that you can keep online

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.540
as you can see it is (indistinct).

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:02.560
I would say the biggest thing

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.030
is to make a schedule, right?

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:05.970
And one of the big things
I offer to the families

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:07.440
of students in my class

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.640
is some kind of first
then picture schedule.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:14.270
So you might have a picture
of first I'm doing Zoom.

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:17.380
Then I get to do a preferred
activity like playdough

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:20.070
or play outside, and
you can draw pictures,

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.260
you can print pictures from the internet.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:24.030
If you wanna find me on social media,

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:26.050
I would be glad to
share with you resources

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:28.530
that I have already made for this.

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:31.030
If you wanna make your life way easier,

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.570
but just helping your kids understand

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:35.440
what the expectation is,
it helps them feel safe

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:38.220
and that helps them be
able to learn better,

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:39.890
be able to regulate their emotions

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:43.020
when it's not something that
they really feel like doing.

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:44.690
But also just to let them know,

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:46.983
hey, here's what I expect for today,

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:48.930
here's what you're going to do

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:50.060
and here's what I'm going to do.

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:52.680
I think that really helps
just having that routine

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.123
and the expectation set.

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:57.940
- Now just watching you, I mean,

00:20:57.940 --> 00:20:59.400
you exude like positive energy,

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.350
but you seem to just have it figured out.

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:05.280
I'm very impressed.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:06.113
There's a question here.

00:21:06.113 --> 00:21:08.060
I don't know if you see
it at the preschool level.

00:21:08.060 --> 00:21:09.730
I could imagine to some
degree from Facebook

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:12.690
Munah Yusuf is asking
how do you encourage kids

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:13.523
to report bullying?

00:21:13.523 --> 00:21:15.090
I guess we could broaden that
to how you deal with bullying

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.350
I'm curious, do you see
bullying at that age group,

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:18.920
at the preschool level,

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:20.870
then how do you deal with it if you do.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:27.080
- I am of the idea that bullying
is still a huge problem.

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:28.710
I think it's a little more invisible

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.110
than it used to be because it's become

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.510
more like cyber bullying or online,

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:36.290
but again, I'll go back
to conscious discipline

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:39.180
there's a great video out
on YouTube about like,

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:41.580
I think it's called "Making
a Bully from Scratch."

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:45.350
And it talks a lot about,
what we do as a society

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:47.930
that encourages that kind of behavior

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:52.930
and what we do and how we help
victims in that situation.

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.830
And so one huge thing we have to work on

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:58.273
is building relationships
between our students.

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:02.370
One thing that Dr. Becky Bailey also says

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:05.880
is that willingness comes from connection,

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.920
and so I'm a lot more likely
to wanna work with you

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.730
and get along with you if
I feel connected to you

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:13.870
and feel like we have something in common.

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:17.290
And so I think it's the job
of teachers at every level

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:21.470
to be building classroom
community within their school.

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:23.630
Like I'm doing this as intentionally,

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:27.400
as I am doing math and
literacy and social studies.

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:31.020
It is a part of every day,
it is a part of every subject

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.435
that we are looking for ways to teach kids

00:22:33.435 --> 00:22:36.060
to see others with love and empathy.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.363
So I think if we could do that,

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:41.290
at every level our kids
would be way better off,

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.653
and I think that the levels
of bullying will go way down.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:49.480
- Yeah, and its a good point,
it starts from someplace

00:22:49.480 --> 00:22:51.300
and the more you can build that early on

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:53.850
the resilient against bullying.

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:57.110
So from YouTube Newtrap Nation ask,

00:22:57.110 --> 00:22:58.510
what do you think about
the way we are learning

00:22:58.510 --> 00:22:59.410
during COVID-19?

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:01.230
And I'll expand that
question a little bit,

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.030
what are you seeing in the students?

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:05.850
I think your students are
very fortunate to have you

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:08.720
and I think you're doing
it as well as anyone can,

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:10.390
but are you seeing some gaps?

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:11.920
Are you worried about even your students

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:14.460
and what are your kind
of concerns more broadly

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:17.513
for what kids are going through right now?

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:22.010
- So I'm troubled by some of the things

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:22.950
that I see happening.

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.850
I think that teachers, administrators,

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:26.980
everyone is doing their absolute best,

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:28.520
but there are just requirements

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:30.180
that we have to meet from our States

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:32.770
and there are requirements
we have to meet federally.

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:36.080
I would like to see some relaxation

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:37.820
on some of those requirements,

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:39.950
because in the situation
of virtual learning,

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:42.710
it is never going to look
just like school does

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:44.130
when you're in person.

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:45.650
And I think the expectations

00:23:45.650 --> 00:23:48.750
that we put on families and on students

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:52.630
to have kids doing this
synchronous learning experience,

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:56.420
six hours a day from home,
it's not sustainable,

00:23:56.420 --> 00:23:59.940
it's not feasible and it's
burning a lot of people out.

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:03.200
Students and families and
teachers all included in that.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:04.440
What I would like to see

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.990
is some more asynchronous opportunities.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:10.500
So say teachers upload
videos of themselves teaching

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:14.490
or lessons or activities and
families are able to complete

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:17.650
those within a week's
time within the timeframe

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:18.793
that works for them.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:21.757
- Yeah, I know what do you
say makes a ton of sense to me

00:24:21.757 --> 00:24:23.290
I mean what we've been saying

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:25.870
when I'm asked the same question is

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:27.570
I'd argue even pre-COVID we were probably

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:30.420
trying to do too much, but
especially during the time

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:32.510
to try to transplant
six hours of in-person

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:35.390
with six hours of video conference.

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:36.860
It's gonna burn out the teachers,

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:37.700
it's gonna burn out the students,

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:38.750
It's gonna burn out the parents,

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:40.300
it's gonna only add anxiety.

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:42.550
This is the time that less is more

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:44.120
so that you have more space

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:47.080
for families to just take
care of everything else

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:48.963
and not atrophy.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.110
Is it just that the teachers
are feeling pressure

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.510
from the state and I
guess then the districts

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:57.030
that hey I gotta cover this many standards

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:59.330
and this many days, and
they're saying you have to have

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:01.970
this much face time in person.

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:05.990
I think some "in person video
conference" makes sense,

00:25:05.990 --> 00:25:08.673
but six hours is ridiculous for anybody.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:12.410
- I think that what they're expecting

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:14.230
is kind of six hours of learning time

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:16.990
and so some teachers
are really able to do,

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:19.250
certain amount of minutes together online,

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:21.110
and then some of the stuff on your own.

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:22.650
The reality we're facing is though

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:24.320
that some students are home alone,

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:26.610
or they don't have someone
helping keep them in a routine

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:29.630
or older siblings or the caregiver.

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:31.290
And so I think some teachers
are feeling pressure

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:34.310
because of that to be more
present in those student lives.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:35.880
But for sure, I think most of the pressure

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.440
is just coming from regulations.

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:41.600
And some schools just
don't have enough staff

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:45.510
to do anything except for let
kids do synchronous learning.

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.410
So I'm gonna be teaching in class,

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:48.500
if you're a remote learner,

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:50.600
you're gonna be watching
me teach in class.

00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:54.220
And so that's where sort of that creates

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:56.728
all of that time online
that is just not healthy

00:25:56.728 --> 00:25:59.980
and not good for kids to
just sit for six hours.

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:01.800
So what I hope is that we're gonna find

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:05.630
some more creative ways to engage online,

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:08.020
but I know that like the hybrid
models that are happening

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:10.400
are some of the most challenging.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.040
- Hmm and we have time for
maybe one last question.

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:15.220
These conversations always
go faster than expected.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:17.487
From Facebook, Maneeba Zeshan asks,

00:26:17.487 --> 00:26:19.917
"How does students bond
interact with fellow classmates

00:26:19.917 --> 00:26:21.347
"in the virtual classroom environment?

00:26:21.347 --> 00:26:23.477
"Our kids are missing
that healthy interaction

00:26:23.477 --> 00:26:26.097
"that they had in class
with their classmates."

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:29.040
- This is also tough.

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:31.600
I love technology in some
ways it makes our world

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:34.563
a lot smaller and some ways
it makes it a lot bigger.

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:37.990
I find the biggest most important thing

00:26:37.990 --> 00:26:39.720
when connecting kids online,

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:41.600
is not just giving them FaceTime with me,

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:45.170
we do need that individual
face time and whole class time,

00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:48.860
but setting up small groups
and I've seen several teachers

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:51.510
do this and they do
things like lunch buddies

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:54.050
and every day of the week

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:55.850
different groups of three or four students

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:57.880
will meet and have lunch together

00:26:57.880 --> 00:27:01.120
then kids are more likely
and that's virtually,

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:03.870
kids are more likely to
open up in a smaller group,

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:06.060
especially our older ones
and with our younger ones,

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:08.260
there are a lot more likely
to be validated and heard

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:10.910
because they have lots of
things to share online.

00:27:10.910 --> 00:27:13.390
And so I think that any
opportunity you have

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:17.990
to decrease the size of the
groups that you're working with,

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:19.170
you should definitely take,

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:22.303
because that is something that
will help build those bonds.

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:26.020
- No, I love that idea, we've
been throwing out the idea

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:29.070
instead of having 60
minutes with 20 or 30 kids,

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:31.980
why not have 10 minutes with
five kids with six kids.

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:33.800
- Yeah, yeah exactly.
- It increases--

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:35.010
The interactivity,

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:36.100
it fits into what you're talking

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:38.121
about around attention span, et cetera.

00:27:38.121 --> 00:27:41.870
We're all out of time I mean,
I could keep talking to you.

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:43.510
And I said, I don't say so you could watch

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:45.580
the other films of other
people that we've...

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:48.870
You naturally exude this
very positive energy

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:52.620
that I think is part of the reason

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:56.570
that you much deservedly our
National Teacher of the Year

00:27:56.570 --> 00:27:57.540
for 2020.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:59.060
Do you have any just final messages

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:01.610
for either parents who
are especially parents

00:28:01.610 --> 00:28:03.400
of four or five year old
struggling right now,

00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:07.080
and teachers who are struggling
with their regular lives

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:10.090
and trying to do distance
learning right now.

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:12.140
- For teachers, the main
thing that I would say

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.000
is use your voice right now.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.980
Okay, we have a big audience,
whether we like it or not,

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:19.780
for the first time, all eyes are on school

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:23.460
and we need you, we need the
aids, we need the custodians

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:26.210
and all that support professionals
to be using their voice,

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:27.840
to talk to the people in their district

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:30.300
about what their needs are and
what their student needs are.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:32.500
We need you to advocate right now

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:35.850
and also hang in there because
you're doing your best.

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:37.820
And so give yourself some grace.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.040
And that's what I wanna
say to families too.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:43.180
This is a big, big, big change

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:44.920
in the way your lives have operated.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.630
And so just give yourself some grace,

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:49.420
do the best that you can,

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:52.830
keep your kids on some kind
of routine and schedule,

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:54.290
but also give them a break

00:28:54.290 --> 00:28:57.830
because this is hard for them
too, but just hang in there.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:00.430
I always say together
we'll see it through.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.433
And I think that's totally true.

00:29:02.433 --> 00:29:05.410
And the more that we work
together and communicate

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.460
and advocate the better off that will be.

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:09.850
- Wow well, that was great advice.

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:12.800
I feel less anxious after you
saying that, so thank you.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:14.650
Tabatha thank you so much and once again,

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:17.120
congratulations on being
National Teacher of the Year,

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:19.023
much, much deserved.

00:29:19.023 --> 00:29:22.089
- Thank you so much for having me.

00:29:22.089 --> 00:29:24.170
- Thank you, well, thanks everyone,

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.620
I think y'all probably felt the same,

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:28.330
positive energy that I felt.

00:29:28.330 --> 00:29:31.100
It's really inspiring to talk
to a teacher like Tabatha.

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:32.290
Thanks so much for joining

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:35.720
and I will see all the
till the next conversation

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:37.520
is going to be next Tuesday.

00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:39.680
We're going to have Lisa Demore

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:42.760
who's a New York times
columnist specializing

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:45.120
on kind of socio-emotional
and emotional wellbeing,

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:46.640
which is obviously a very important topic.

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.140
Some of which we just touched on.

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:51.530
So I will see you on Tuesday
on the Homeroom Livestream.

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:52.363
See you then.

